VALUES ('ARMK','published','Food, hospitality and facilities-services company serving institutions, workplaces and leisure venues.','Aramark operates outsourced food and facilities programs across education, healthcare, business, corrections, sports and entertainment. Contract retention, new-business wins, labor productivity, purchasing scale and client reimbursement terms determine owner economics.','Dining, catering, refreshments, concessions, hospitality and facilities-management services.','Multi-year client contracts generate management fees, per-person charges, product sales and reimbursable operating revenue, with economics varying by contract structure.','Institutions can outsource more non-core operations, while pricing, procurement scale, menu innovation and venue participation can raise revenue and margins.','Aramark must retain clients, price labor and food inflation, recruit and schedule employees, execute venue volumes and convert new contracts without sacrificing service quality.','Track organic revenue separately from pass-through inflation, then reconcile retention, net new business, labor costs, food costs, working capital and capital invested in client locations.','Scale and retention can compound service cash flow','["Long client relationships and embedded operations support recurring demand.","Procurement and operating scale can improve unit economics.","Outsourcing and premium venue experiences can expand the addressable market."]','Thin margins and labor intensity limit error tolerance','["Wage and food inflation can outrun contract pricing.","Client losses or weak venue attendance can reduce fixed-cost absorption.","Service failures, safety issues or labor disruption can damage renewals."]','The thesis breaks if retention or net new business weakens while inflation and contract investment keep margins and cash conversion structurally below the cost of capital','[{"label":"RETENTION / NEW BUSINESS","value":"Contract engine","detail":"Renewals, wins, losses, ramp costs and client concentration"},{"label":"PRICING / PRODUCTIVITY","value":"Margin engine","detail":"Labor, food, procurement, contract pass-throughs and operating efficiency"},{"label":"CASH CONVERSION","value":"Owner outcome","detail":"Working capital, client-location investment, capital spending and free cash flow"}]','[{"title":"Labor and food inflation","level":"High","text":"Employee and input costs can rise before pricing or productivity offsets them.","signal":"Wage rates, food basket, pricing and segment margin"},{"title":"Contract execution","level":"High","text":"Large client losses, poor openings or service failures can impair revenue and reputation.","signal":"Retention, net wins, startup costs and customer concentration"},{"title":"Volume exposure","level":"Medium","text":"Attendance and occupancy affect education, business and venue demand.","signal":"Per-capita spending, attendance, occupancy and same-site sales"}]','[{"title":"Contract momentum","text":"Track retention, net new wins and the cost and timing of new-account ramps.","impact":"BULL CASE"},{"title":"Margin and cash discipline","text":"Require pricing and productivity to convert organic growth into operating cash after client investment.","impact":"NEUTRAL"}]','["How much organic growth comes from volume, price and net new contracts?","Which contracts require the most upfront capital or working capital?","Are labor productivity and retention improving together?"]','Compass Group, Sodexo, Delaware North, ABM Industries, self-operated institutional programs and local service providers.','2025-11-25','2026-08-04T21:06:00Z',CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)
