VALUES ('ARMP','published','Clinical-stage biotechnology company developing pathogen-specific bacteriophage therapeutics for serious bacterial infections.','Armata develops phage cocktails including Pseudomonas-focused AP-PA02 and AP-PA03 and Staphylococcus aureus candidate AP-SA02. Clinical evidence, manufacturing consistency, regulatory alignment and financing—not current product sales—drive the owner case.','Investigational bacteriophage therapeutics and a phage discovery, development and manufacturing platform.','The company has no approved commercial product; future economics depend on regulatory approvals, product sales, licenses, partnerships, grants and milestone or royalty arrangements.','Antimicrobial resistance and difficult chronic or systemic infections create demand for therapies that can target bacteria differently from conventional antibiotics.','Candidates must demonstrate safety and efficacy, phage manufacturing must scale reproducibly, regulators must accept development plans and the company must finance trials without destructive dilution.','Separate scientific promise from investable evidence: track enrolled patients, endpoints, FDA interactions, manufacturing readiness, grant support, Innoviva-related financing and cash runway.','Targeted phage therapy could address resistant infections','["Pathogen-specific cocktails offer a differentiated mechanism against difficult bacteria.","Government and strategic funding can validate programs and reduce some development cost.","Positive controlled clinical evidence could create platform and partnering value."]','Binary development and financing risks remain dominant','["Clinical trials may not confirm efficacy or may face enrollment and endpoint challenges.","Complex biologic manufacturing can delay or limit reproducibility.","Dependence on external financing and a concentrated strategic holder can dilute or constrain owners."]','The thesis breaks if lead programs fail to produce reproducible clinical benefit or manufacturing and financing limitations prevent completion of pivotal development','[{"label":"CLINICAL EVIDENCE","value":"Primary value driver","detail":"Enrollment, endpoints, safety, durability and regulatory feedback for AP-PA and AP-SA programs"},{"label":"MANUFACTURING","value":"Platform test","detail":"Phage selection, potency, purity, yield, release testing and commercial-scale readiness"},{"label":"CASH RUNWAY","value":"Survival constraint","detail":"Cash burn, grants, debt, strategic funding, milestones and diluted share count"}]','[{"title":"Clinical and regulatory","level":"High","text":"Early signals may not translate into pivotal approval evidence.","signal":"Trial design, enrollment, endpoints, adverse events and FDA feedback"},{"title":"Manufacturing","level":"High","text":"Phage cocktails require consistent biologic production and quality control.","signal":"Batch success, capacity, comparability and release testing"},{"title":"Financing and control","level":"High","text":"Losses require capital and strategic financing may add dilution, debt or governance constraints.","signal":"Runway, borrowing, ownership concentration and share issuance"}]','[{"title":"Lead-program proof","text":"Require transparent controlled data and regulator-agreed next steps for AP-SA02 and Pseudomonas programs.","impact":"BULL CASE"},{"title":"Runway versus milestones","text":"Compare cash needs and financing terms with the time required to reach value-changing evidence.","impact":"BEAR CASE"}]','["What evidence establishes benefit beyond standard antibiotics?","Can the current facility reproducibly supply pivotal and commercial-scale phage cocktails?","How much dilution or debt is required to reach the next regulator-agreed milestone?"]','Adaptive Phage Therapeutics, BiomX, Locus Biosciences, conventional antibiotics and other anti-infective biotechnology companies.','2026-03-25','2026-08-04T21:06:00Z',CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)
