VALUES ('AROC','published','U.S. provider of natural-gas contract compression and aftermarket services.','Archrock owns and operates compression equipment that moves natural gas through production and midstream systems, and services customer-owned equipment. Installed horsepower, utilization, contract pricing, maintenance reliability and capital intensity shape returns.','Natural-gas compression capacity under service contracts plus parts, maintenance, overhaul and field services for customer-owned equipment.','Contract operations produce recurring monthly service revenue; aftermarket services earn project, labor and parts revenue.','Growing gas production, LNG-related demand and infrastructure bottlenecks can support high utilization and pricing for compression fleets.','Archrock must deploy capital into attractive equipment, maintain uptime, manage customer and basin exposure, fund growth prudently and preserve safety and environmental performance.','Revenue durability must be judged alongside fleet age, horsepower utilization, maintenance capital, growth capital, customer credit, leverage and free cash flow after equipment spending.','Scarce compression capacity can support durable cash flow','["Compression is essential infrastructure for gathering and transporting natural gas.","High utilization can improve pricing and fleet economics.","A large service network and installed fleet create customer and operating advantages."]','Capital intensity and cycle exposure can erode returns','["Equipment additions can destroy value if gas activity or pricing weakens.","Customer distress or basin concentration can reduce utilization.","Debt, maintenance failures, safety events and emissions rules can increase cost."]','The thesis breaks if utilization and pricing fall while maintenance, growth capital and leverage keep free cash flow and returns below sustainable levels','[{"label":"HORSEPOWER / UTILIZATION","value":"Volume base","detail":"Active fleet, deployed horsepower, idle equipment, basins and customer demand"},{"label":"RATE / UPTIME","value":"Revenue quality","detail":"Contract pricing, duration, availability, maintenance and service mix"},{"label":"CAPITAL / LEVERAGE","value":"Owner conversion","detail":"Maintenance and growth capital, free cash flow, debt and distributions"}]','[{"title":"Energy and customer cycle","level":"High","text":"Gas activity and customer financial health affect fleet demand and collections.","signal":"Utilization, customer concentration, basin activity and receivables"},{"title":"Capital intensity","level":"High","text":"New equipment and overhauls require cash before returns are certain.","signal":"Capital spending, backlog, return thresholds and free cash flow"},{"title":"Operations and regulation","level":"Medium","text":"Equipment failures, safety events and emissions rules can disrupt service or raise cost.","signal":"Uptime, incidents, maintenance expense and compliance capital"}]','[{"title":"Fleet economics","text":"Track horsepower, utilization, rate, uptime and capital required per incremental cash-flow dollar.","impact":"BULL CASE"},{"title":"Balance-sheet discipline","text":"Reconcile growth spending and shareholder distributions with leverage and through-cycle cash coverage.","impact":"NEUTRAL"}]','["How much pricing is protected by contract duration?","What return is expected on newly ordered compression equipment?","How sensitive is free cash flow to utilization and maintenance capital?"]','Kodiak Gas Services, USA Compression Partners, CSI Compressco and customer-owned compression fleets.','2026-02-26','2026-08-04T21:06:00Z',CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)
