VALUES ('AROW','published','Regional financial holding company operating Arrow Bank and related wealth and insurance services in upstate New York.','Arrow Financial gathers local deposits and lends to consumers and businesses while earning fee income from trust, wealth and insurance activities. Net interest margin, deposit franchise quality, credit losses, capital and disciplined expansion determine owner value.','Deposit accounts, residential and commercial loans, consumer lending, cash-management, trust, wealth-management and insurance services.','Net interest income from loans and securities funded by deposits and borrowings, plus service, trust, wealth and insurance fees.','A stable community deposit base, relationship banking, fee businesses and selective market or acquisition expansion can compound earnings and book value.','Arrow must retain low-cost deposits, price loans for risk, control credit losses, manage interest-rate exposure, integrate expansion and maintain regulatory capital and compliance.','For a bank, reported earnings are incomplete without deposit mix and cost, securities marks, nonperforming assets, charge-offs, loan concentrations, liquidity and tangible capital.','A relationship deposit franchise can compound locally','["Long-standing market relationships can support stable deposits and cross-selling.","Trust, wealth and insurance fees diversify spread income.","Disciplined credit and expansion can grow tangible book value per share."]','Rate and credit mistakes can overwhelm steady growth','["Deposit competition can compress net interest margin.","Commercial, residential or consumer credit losses can rise after benign periods.","Regulation, cyber risk and acquisition integration increase operating demands."]','The thesis breaks if deposit outflows, funding costs or credit losses cause sustained deterioration in tangible capital and per-share book value','[{"label":"DEPOSITS / NIM","value":"Core earnings","detail":"Deposit balances, noninterest-bearing mix, funding cost, asset yield and net interest margin"},{"label":"CREDIT QUALITY","value":"Downside control","detail":"Nonperforming loans, delinquencies, charge-offs, allowance and loan concentrations"},{"label":"CAPITAL / BOOK VALUE","value":"Owner compounding","detail":"Tangible equity, regulatory ratios, share count, dividends and acquisition economics"}]','[{"title":"Interest-rate and liquidity","level":"High","text":"Deposit repricing and securities or loan duration can pressure margin and liquidity.","signal":"Deposit beta, uninsured deposits, borrowings, accumulated other comprehensive loss and NIM"},{"title":"Credit","level":"High","text":"Local economic weakness can increase losses in concentrated loan books.","signal":"Delinquencies, criticized loans, nonperformers, charge-offs and reserve coverage"},{"title":"Regulatory and operational","level":"Medium","text":"Compliance, cyber events and integration failures can raise cost or constrain growth.","signal":"Consent orders, incidents, expense growth and merger milestones"}]','[{"title":"Deposit franchise","text":"Track account retention, mix, pricing and liquidity rather than headline deposit growth alone.","impact":"BULL CASE"},{"title":"Per-share capital","text":"Require earnings, dividends and expansion to increase tangible book value without weakening credit reserves.","impact":"NEUTRAL"}]','["How much of margin recovery depends on deposit repricing?","Which loan concentrations carry the most loss sensitivity?","Will expansion improve tangible book value per share after integration costs?"]','Community Bank System, NBT Bancorp, TrustCo Bank, KeyCorp and other regional and community banks in upstate New York.','2026-03-06','2026-08-04T21:06:00Z',CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)
