VALUES ('ARQT','published','Commercial-stage medical dermatology company centered on topical roflumilast products sold under the ZORYVE brand.','Arcutis develops and commercializes advanced topical therapies for chronic inflammatory skin diseases. ZORYVE demand, indication expansion, reimbursement, prescriber adoption, gross margin and pipeline concentration drive the owner outcome.','Prescription topical foam and cream formulations of roflumilast for approved dermatology indications, plus related clinical-development programs.','Product sales through specialty distribution and pharmacies, net of rebates, discounts and returns; future pipeline products or partnerships may add revenue.','Additional age groups, formulations and inflammatory-skin indications can extend the ZORYVE franchise and commercial platform.','Arcutis must sustain prescriptions and access, secure approvals for new indications, manufacture reliably, protect intellectual property and convert rapid growth into durable cash generation.','Track prescription demand and net revenue per prescription separately; payer rebates, free-goods programs, inventory, indication mix and commercial spending determine whether adoption creates owner value.','A single dermatology platform can expand across indications','["Established PDE4 biology and differentiated topical formulations support physician adoption.","Multiple approved indications can leverage one commercial organization.","Further label and age expansion can broaden the treated population."]','Franchise concentration magnifies commercial setbacks','["Revenue depends heavily on ZORYVE and payer access.","Competing branded, generic and biologic therapies can limit uptake or pricing.","Patent, safety, manufacturing or regulatory setbacks can impair the whole platform."]','The thesis breaks if ZORYVE prescription growth fails to produce sustainable net revenue and cash generation or if reimbursement and competition block profitable indication expansion','[{"label":"ZORYVE DEMAND","value":"Commercial proof","detail":"Prescriptions, new and repeat patients, prescribers, indication and formulation mix"},{"label":"NET PRICE / ACCESS","value":"Revenue quality","detail":"Coverage, prior authorization, rebates, discounts, free goods and net revenue per prescription"},{"label":"MARGIN / CASH","value":"Owner conversion","detail":"Gross margin, commercial spending, inventory, operating cash flow and runway"}]','[{"title":"Product concentration","level":"High","text":"Most value depends on one active ingredient and brand family.","signal":"ZORYVE share of revenue, safety reports and label progress"},{"title":"Access and competition","level":"High","text":"Payers and alternative therapies can restrict use or net pricing.","signal":"Covered lives, rejection rates, rebate rate and competitive launches"},{"title":"Regulatory and manufacturing","level":"Medium","text":"Approval delays, quality failures or supply constraints can interrupt growth.","signal":"FDA actions, batch release, inventory and shortages"}]','[{"title":"Commercial durability","text":"Track repeat prescriptions, coverage and net revenue across each approved ZORYVE indication.","impact":"BULL CASE"},{"title":"Cash inflection","text":"Require gross profit growth to outpace commercial and development spending on the path to self-funding.","impact":"NEUTRAL"}]','["How much demand is repeat use versus launch sampling?","What net price remains after payer rebates and patient support?","Can the same commercial infrastructure profitably support new indications?"]','Incyte, Eli Lilly, Pfizer, Organon, Bristol Myers Squibb, topical generics, biologics and other dermatology therapies.','2026-02-25','2026-08-04T21:06:00Z',CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)
