VALUES ('ARTV','published','Clinical-stage biotechnology company developing off-the-shelf natural-killer-cell therapies.','Artiva develops cryopreserved allogeneic NK-cell therapies led by AlloNK, also known as AB-101, for use with B-cell-targeting antibodies in autoimmune disease and cancer. Clinical response, safety, cell expansion, manufacturing consistency and runway define value.','Investigational donor-derived NK-cell therapy candidates and the manufacturing platform used to produce cryopreserved doses.','The company has no approved commercial product; potential economics depend on approvals, product sales, collaborations, licenses, milestones and royalties.','An off-the-shelf NK-cell product that works with established antibodies could offer scalable immune-cell therapy without patient-specific manufacturing.','AlloNK must show reproducible benefit and acceptable safety, combination regimens must be practical, manufacturing must scale and cash must last through regulator-agreed milestones.','Evaluate complete patient-level durability, safety and dose evidence rather than response headlines; also track manufacturing yield, batch comparability, enrollment, collaboration dependence and diluted runway.','Off-the-shelf NK cells could broaden cell therapy','["Cryopreserved allogeneic doses may simplify treatment logistics.","Combination with established antibodies provides a clear biologic strategy.","Proof across autoimmune disease and oncology could validate a reusable platform."]','Early evidence and manufacturing risk make outcomes binary','["Small uncontrolled studies may not predict pivotal benefit.","Cell persistence, variability or combination safety may limit efficacy.","Long development timelines and no product revenue create financing and dilution risk."]','The thesis breaks if controlled evidence fails to show durable clinical benefit or if manufacturing and financing cannot support pivotal development','[{"label":"CLINICAL RESPONSE","value":"Lead proof","detail":"Complete response, durability, relapse, steroid use, safety and patient selection"},{"label":"CELL PRODUCT","value":"Platform proof","detail":"Expansion, persistence, potency, lot consistency, release success and dose availability"},{"label":"RUNWAY","value":"Financing constraint","detail":"Cash burn, trial pace, collaborations, milestone timing and diluted shares"}]','[{"title":"Clinical translation","level":"High","text":"Early response data may not replicate in larger controlled studies.","signal":"Patient count, controls, durability, adverse events and protocol changes"},{"title":"Cell manufacturing","level":"High","text":"Donor-derived cell products require consistent potency and supply.","signal":"Yield, failed lots, comparability, capacity and cost per dose"},{"title":"Financing","level":"High","text":"The company needs capital before commercial revenue is possible.","signal":"Runway, burn, offerings, collaboration cash and share count"}]','[{"title":"AlloNK evidence","text":"Track complete enrolled cohorts, durability and safety by indication and combination partner.","impact":"BULL CASE"},{"title":"Manufacturing and runway","text":"Require lot consistency and sufficient cash to reach regulator-agreed comparative evidence.","impact":"BEAR CASE"}]','["What controlled evidence will distinguish AlloNK contribution from the antibody partner?","How consistent are potency and expansion across manufactured lots?","Which milestone can be reached with existing cash without material dilution?"]','Fate Therapeutics, Nkarta, Century Therapeutics, Caribou Biosciences, autologous CAR-T therapies and antibody-only regimens.','2026-03-10','2026-08-04T21:06:00Z',CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)
