VALUES ('ARVN','published','Clinical-stage biotechnology company developing PROTAC protein degraders for oncology and neuroscience.','Arvinas uses its targeted-protein-degradation platform to design medicines that remove disease-causing proteins. Program-specific clinical evidence, partner economics, intellectual property, pipeline prioritization and cash runway drive the owner case.','Investigational PROTAC protein degraders and a discovery platform for oncology, neurologic and other diseases.','Before product approval, economics come from collaborations, upfront and milestone payments, research funding and future royalties; approved products could add direct or shared sales.','Targeted degradation may address proteins that conventional inhibitors cannot control and may create differentiated efficacy across multiple diseases.','Programs must show durable clinical benefit, tolerable safety and practical dosing; partnerships must preserve value, the platform must generate follow-on assets and capital must reach decisive milestones.','Do not treat platform breadth as value without program-level proof. Track enrolled patients, comparator context, partner rights, milestone probability, R&D prioritization, cash burn and diluted ownership.','PROTAC validation could create a repeatable drug engine','["Targeted degradation offers a distinct mechanism from occupancy-based drugs.","Clinical proof in one target can strengthen confidence in platform design rules.","Partnerships can fund development and provide commercial capabilities."]','Pipeline setbacks and partner dependence can reset value','["Clinical candidates can fail for efficacy, safety or pharmacology.","Partners can reprioritize, return rights or control key development decisions.","Long timelines, patents and financing needs create dilution and execution risk."]','The thesis breaks if lead degraders fail to show differentiated clinical benefit and the platform cannot repeatedly produce partnerable assets before capital is exhausted','[{"label":"CLINICAL DIFFERENTIATION","value":"Program proof","detail":"Response, progression, durability, safety, dosing and comparator context"},{"label":"PARTNER ECONOMICS","value":"Value share","detail":"Rights, cost sharing, milestones, royalties, opt-outs and development control"},{"label":"PIPELINE / RUNWAY","value":"Platform survival","detail":"Candidate generation, prioritization, cash burn, milestones and diluted shares"}]','[{"title":"Clinical development","level":"High","text":"Mechanistic promise may not translate into differentiated patient outcomes.","signal":"Complete data, controls, discontinuations, safety and regulator feedback"},{"title":"Collaboration dependence","level":"High","text":"Partner strategy and contractual rights can change program value.","signal":"Opt-ins, returned rights, cost sharing, milestones and trial ownership"},{"title":"Platform and financing","level":"High","text":"Multiple programs consume capital before repeatability is proven.","signal":"R&D spend, cash runway, program cuts, patents and share issuance"}]','[{"title":"Program-level proof","text":"Require durable, comparator-aware evidence for each lead degrader rather than extrapolating from mechanism.","impact":"BULL CASE"},{"title":"Capital allocation","text":"Track which programs receive funding, what rights remain and whether runway reaches decisive data.","impact":"NEUTRAL"}]','["What clinical outcome proves degradation is better than inhibition for each target?","How do partner rights and cost sharing affect retained economics?","Can the platform deliver another differentiated candidate before additional financing?"]','Nurix Therapeutics, C4 Therapeutics, Kymera Therapeutics, Foghorn Therapeutics, conventional targeted drugs and other protein-degradation platforms.','2026-02-24','2026-08-04T21:06:00Z',CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)
