VALUES ('ARW','published','Global technology distributor serving electronic-components and enterprise-computing markets.','Arrow Electronics connects semiconductor and technology suppliers with manufacturers, solution providers and enterprise customers. Sales volume is large, but product mix, supplier terms, working capital, inventory risk and value-added services determine thin-margin owner returns.','Semiconductors and other electronic components, enterprise hardware and software, cloud and lifecycle solutions, engineering support and supply-chain services.','Product distribution and resale revenue plus value-added engineering, integration, cloud, logistics and services economics across global components and enterprise computing.','Design support, complex supply chains, cloud marketplaces and intelligent solutions can deepen relationships and add higher-value services to distribution scale.','Arrow must manage inventory and receivables through cycles, preserve supplier franchises, comply with export rules, protect thin margins and convert reported earnings into cash.','Revenue is not the best scorecard for a distributor. Track gross profit dollars, segment margin, inventory turns, receivable quality, supplier concentration, working capital and returns on invested capital.','Scale and engineering services can make distribution more valuable','["Broad supplier and customer networks provide reach and purchasing scale.","Design-chain and engineering support can influence component selection early.","Enterprise cloud and lifecycle services can add recurring, higher-value economics."]','Thin margins expose every working-capital mistake','["Semiconductor cycles can leave excess or obsolete inventory.","Suppliers may change authorizations, allocations, rebates or direct-sales strategies.","Export controls, customer credit and price competition can impair revenue and cash."]','The thesis breaks if volume growth repeatedly consumes working capital without stable gross profit, cash conversion and adequate returns on invested capital','[{"label":"GROSS PROFIT / MIX","value":"Economic output","detail":"Components versus enterprise mix, services, rebates, pricing and segment margin"},{"label":"WORKING CAPITAL","value":"Cash engine","detail":"Inventory turns, receivable days, payables, provisions and operating cash flow"},{"label":"FRANCHISE / COMPLIANCE","value":"Durability","detail":"Supplier authorizations, customer concentration, export controls and geographic exposure"}]','[{"title":"Technology cycle and inventory","level":"High","text":"Demand corrections can create excess inventory and pricing pressure.","signal":"Inventory days, reserves, cancellations, lead times and book-to-bill"},{"title":"Supplier and customer power","level":"High","text":"Large counterparties can alter terms, allocations or channel strategy.","signal":"Concentration, rebates, direct sales and credit losses"},{"title":"Trade and compliance","level":"High","text":"Export controls and sanctions can restrict products, customers and geographies.","signal":"Entity-list actions, licenses, investigations and geographic sales"}]','[{"title":"Cash conversion","text":"Compare earnings with working-capital movement and normalized operating cash across the component cycle.","impact":"NEUTRAL"},{"title":"Value-added mix","text":"Track whether engineering, cloud and lifecycle services improve gross profit and customer retention.","impact":"BULL CASE"}]','["How much gross profit comes from value-added services versus product availability?","Are inventory and receivable risks rising faster than sales?","How exposed are supplier franchises and customers to export restrictions?"]','Avnet, TD SYNNEX, Ingram Micro, WPG Holdings, DigiKey, Mouser, direct supplier sales and cloud marketplaces.','2026-02-11','2026-08-04T21:06:00Z',CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)
